  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 245 PM EDT Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Cool and wet weather continues this afternoon. \nGradually drying out tonight.\n\nThis afternoon, a frontal boundary is located just to the E/SE of \nthe local area (along the SC and NC coasts) with weak areas of low \npressure moving along it. Meanwhile, high pressure over eastern New \nEngland continues to wedge south into the Mid Atlantic, reinforcing \na cool/overrunning layer across the local CWA. Widespread light to \nmoderate rain currently stretches from central/eastern VA over to \nthe Eastern Shore and down into NE NC. Temperatures range from the \nlow-mid 60s NW to the low 70s across the far SE. Rain will taper off \nfrom NW to SE later this afternoon and evening as the coastal front \nmoves further offshore and drier air gradually filters in. Clouds \nwill be slow to clear and mostly cloudy to overcast skies are \nexpected to persist through tonight. Low temperatures \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Seasonable temperatures and dry this week.\n\nCold advection will initially lag behind the secondary front on \nMonday. So, temps will actually be warmer than this weekend for most \nlocations as the sun makes a reappearance. Highs should be in the \n70s for all of our CWA, warmest W of the Chesapeake Bay. The more \npronounced cooler and drier air arrives Monday evening into Monday \nnight as ~1025 mb high pressure builds down toward the Mid-Atlantic \nfrom the Great Lakes region. Markedly cooler Monday night as lows \ndip into the 50s for SE VA/NE NC, with mid to upper 40s elsewhere. \nThe cooler/dry theme continues into the midweek period as the high \npressure system builds overhead. Tuesday should feel most like fall \nwith highs in the upper 60s to lower 70s inland (low-mid 60s Eastern \nShore). Similarly, Tuesday's overnight lows will also be the coolest \nof the week. The current forecast has 40s for most of the area, \nthough a light onshore wind near the coast likely keeps these areas \nin the 50s. Should also note that some upper 30s are not out of the \nrealm of possibility, especially for rural and typically cooler \nlocations in the Piedmont. \n\nUpper-level heights rebound some by Wednesday and a gradual warming \ntrend is expected through the end of the week. Subsidence and a very \ndry 700-300 mb layer will also keep everyone dry. The next chance of \nrain is not until next Saturday night or Sunday, but the exact \ndetails still need to be ironed out.",
